<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Greater Caucasus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Greater <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> is the main range of the Caucasus Mountains, running about 1,200 kilometres from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the Caspian and rising to 5,642 metres at Mount Elbrus. Its passes, especially the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darial-pass">Darial Pass</a>, and the coastal route at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> were the gateways through which steppe peoples entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a>, and empires from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> sought to control them.</p></div>
